      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:07 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Brian T. Burgess (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Fifth Circuit's decision should be reversed for either of two independent reasons. First, a Rule 59(e) motion filed within 20 days of judgment is part of the first full opportunity to pursue habeas relief. It is not a second habeas application."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0000-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We'll hear argument next in Case 18-6943, Banister versus Davis."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think the flip side of that I do want to comment on is that we believe the other side's position is much -- going to be much more inefficient, sort of perversely, even though they argue that their approach is designed to streamline the -- the habeas review process, because, under their approach, any time a Rule 59(e) motion is filed, a judge cannot just look at it and say: I don't see anything here. Denied. Instead, the judge has to make a threshold inquiry to determine, is there something that constitutes a habeas claim within the meaning of Gonzalez? In some instances, that might be simple; in others, not so much."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: But AEDPA was intended to move habeas petitions along quickly and is full of deadlines. But there is no deadline for a ruling on a 59(e) motion. Isn't that an anomaly?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Gorsuch",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 32,
        "start": 32
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GORSUCH: Or it might have taken the district court no time at all. I mean, you -- you file a stylized play, a thousand pages of a stylized play, twice, I would think the second time around, the district court might be righteously indignant and have very little trouble denying that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "FCC versus League of Women Voters that if you file a 59(e) motion that doesn't go to the objects of Rule 59, that it's not actually filed, and it doesn't extend your notice of appeal deadline. And the courts of appeals have recognized that as well in 59(e) contexts. For example, the Tenth Circuit, in an opinion by Judge McConnell in a case called Allender versus Raytheon, noted that a Rule 59(e) motion that did not comply with Rule 7's pleading requirements is not actually filed and does not have any effect on the notice of appeal deadline."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But the pre--- the pre-AEDPA practice was to treat 59 and 60 differently. So you would expect some clear indication, I think, from Congress if they were going to upend that long-standing practice in repeating the \"second or successive\" language. And you started your argument by saying there's no difference between 59 and 60. But there's the 28-day time period and there is the pre-AEDPA history, where the lower courts really did distinguish the two in this context. So how do you respond to that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAVANAUGH: How about an amended complaint then?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What do you do with the merger that -- that this is treated -- if the motion is denied, it merges into the final judgment?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: So is your -- is it your -- is -- do I have this right or not? In your -- in your view, on day 42, after the original complaint was filed and they had a trial and hearing and so forth, judgment comes in. The lawyer reads it. Next day, he files a piece of paper."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Texas",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The last point I'd like to make is with regard to Rule 4(a) and to Texas's argument about that. Their argument, as I understand it, relies on examples that exclusively involve instances that are not seeking genuine Rule 59(e) relief and you need to look beyond the pleading to that -- to at least that extent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Texas",
      "case_id": "2019-18-6943",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "d918c3b7ea5b31e1fa858748f7dc2f8932f2387da5c8517bda3277784e5c3156",
      "evidence_id": "ev-d918c3b7ea5b-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But Texas has expressly conceded -- this is at page 44 of their brief -- that Mr. Banister did file a true Rule 59(e) motion. They just think it should be subject to Section 2244(b)."
    },
    {
